# Sigvara oracle.
#
# Arc testnet (5042002) is the current target — see docs/arc.md.
#   RPC_URL=https://rpc.testnet.arc.io
#   gas is USDC (18 decimals on-chain); fund the oracle wallet at https://faucet.circle.com
#   addresses come from deployments/5042002.json after you run Deploy.s.sol

RPC_URL=https://rpc.testnet.arc.io

# The chain this oracle is for: 5042 is Arc mainnet, 5042002 is the testnet. Set it to match
# RPC_URL. With it set, nothing is read for scoring or signed while the RPC reports another
# chain, and a state file saved on another chain is refused. Unset, the oracle signs for
# whatever chain RPC_URL serves and warns at startup, so a testnet URL beside mainnet
# addresses, or an endpoint that fails over onto another chain, is not caught.
EXPECTED_CHAIN_ID=5042002

# Oracle wallet private key — must have ORACLE_ROLE on SigvaraReputation
# Generate a fresh wallet: cast wallet new
ORACLE_PRIVATE_KEY=0x...

# Contract addresses — fill from deployments/<chainId>.json
IDENTITY_ADDRESS=0x...
REPUTATION_ADDRESS=0x...

# Epoch interval. Set to 1 for testnet fast feedback, 24 for production cadence.
EPOCH_HOURS=1

# HTTP bind address — defaults to 127.0.0.1 (localhost only) for bare-metal runs.
# In Docker set HOST=0.0.0.0: docker-compose.oracle.yml publishes the port as
# 127.0.0.1:3030 (that mapping is what restricts access to the host), and the app
# must bind 0.0.0.0 INSIDE the container or the published port can't reach it.
# Only expose beyond localhost with ORACLE_ADMIN_TOKEN set.
HOST=127.0.0.1

# HTTP port
PORT=3030

# Block to start scanning AgentRegistered events from: the deploy block minus a small
# buffer (cast block-number --rpc-url arc_testnet around deploy time).
FROM_BLOCK=0

# Max blocks per eth_getLogs call.
LOG_CHUNK_SIZE=2000

# Bearer token protecting /attest, /flag, /link, and /epoch from public writes.
# If unset, those endpoints are open (fine for local testing, not for a public VPS —
# the server logs a warning at startup). Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
ORACLE_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# Per-service write credentials. Each ORACLE_TOKEN_<NAME> is an independent token, and
# <NAME> is what the oracle logs when that caller writes.
#
#   ORACLE_TOKEN_COUNTERAUDIT=...
#   ORACLE_TOKEN_HOODSCAN=...
#
# One shared token is fine while the operator is the only writer, and stops being a
# secret the moment a second service holds it: revoking one integration then means
# rotating every integration, so in practice nobody revokes anything, and the write log
# cannot say which service acted. Give each integration its own.
#
# To revoke one, delete or empty its line and recreate the container (`docker compose up -d`;
# a plain restart keeps the old environment). The startup banner lists the
# credential names it loaded (never the values), which is where you confirm it went.
# ORACLE_ADMIN_TOKEN still works, as the credential named `admin`.
#
# Generate each with: openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Restrict a credential to some of the writes with ORACLE_SCOPES_<NAME>, a comma-separated
# list of: attest (record outcomes), flag (/flag and /flag/resolve), link, epoch. With no
# such line a credential may do everything. admin is never restricted. A scope that does not
# exist, or a restriction on a credential that does not exist, stops the process at startup.
#
#   ORACLE_SCOPES_COUNTERAUDIT=attest

# Peers whose X-Forwarded-For header is believed, besides loopback: addresses or CIDR ranges,
# comma-separated. Behind a reverse proxy in a container the proxy reaches this process from a
# Docker bridge address, so list the proxy network; without it every visitor shares one
# rate-limit bucket and one client can make the rest, the watcher included, get 429s. Do not
# list a range that includes untrusted clients.
#
#   TRUSTED_PROXIES=172.20.0.0/16
#
# Use the subnet of the ONE network the proxy shares with this container (docker network
# inspect <network>), not the whole 172.16.0.0/12 private range: every container in the range
# would be allowed to pick the address it is rate-limited as.

# Path to the JSON state file (attestations, flags, links, cooldowns).
# Must be writable; parent directories are created automatically.
# In Docker, mount a volume to this directory for persistence across restarts.
ORACLE_STATE_PATH=/data/oracle-state.json
#
# The state file also holds the AgentRegistered log scan cursor, so a restart
# resumes rather than replaying the chain from FROM_BLOCK. Delete the file and the
# next start rescans from FROM_BLOCK, which is the recovery path if the cursor is
# ever wrong, but on a busy chain that scan can be large enough for a public RPC
# to rate limit it.

# The state file is read before anything else happens. One that exists and cannot be read
# stops the process rather than being replaced by an empty one: it holds what cannot be
# rebuilt from the chain. Each save keeps the one before it as <path>.bak, and an unreadable
# file is recovered from that, with /health reporting stateSource "backup". Copy the file
# off the host on a schedule as well; the .bak is on the same disk.

# Minimum milliseconds before the same attester can re-attest the same agent.
# Prevents attestation spam and score inflation. Default: 3600000 (1 hour).
# Set to 0 to disable cooldowns (not recommended for production).
ATTEST_COOLDOWN_MS=3600000

# ---- Transactions and the clock ---------------------------------------------
# The RPC chooses the fees it quotes, whether a transaction has been mined, and what time the
# chain says it is. These bound how far the oracle takes its word for each. Every numeric
# setting in this file is checked at startup: a value that is set and is not a plain number
# in range stops the process, with the setting named. A blank value means unset.
#
# Seconds to wait for a transaction to be mined. After that it is replaced once, with the
# same nonce at twice the fee, and waited for again; if that does not land either, the epoch
# moves on and the next one reads the chain as it is. 5 to 3600.
TX_WAIT_SECONDS=60

# The most the oracle will sign for one transaction. A transaction over either is refused
# before it is signed, and costs nothing. MAX_FEE_GWEI caps the price of gas (Arc's minimum
# base fee is 20 gwei, so 1000 is far above normal; tighten it after watching real fees).
# MAX_TX_COST caps price times gas limit, in the native token (USDC on Arc), which also
# catches an inflated gas estimate.
MAX_FEE_GWEI=1000
MAX_TX_COST=1

# How far the chain's latest block time may be from this host's clock, in seconds, before
# the epoch is not run. Payments are dated and scores aged by the block time the RPC reports,
# so a node serving a stale or false head moves every score the same way, unseen.
MAX_CLOCK_SKEW_SECONDS=600

# ---- Payment-backed attestations -------------------------------------------
# With verification off, /attest is an unauthenticated POST carrying whatever
# `attester` string the caller chose, and feeScore is attestation count divided
# by ten. That makes the largest factor in the score a count of HTTP requests.
#
# Set to "required" and an attestation must reference the settlement transaction
# of a real payment to the agent's own address: the `transaction` field of an
# x402 X-PAYMENT-RESPONSE, or any other transfer that settled on chain. The
# oracle reads the receipt, takes the payer from the transfer itself, credits the
# settlement once, and computes feeScore from measured volume.
#
# Default "off" so existing testnet deployments keep working. Mainnet should be
# "required": scores mean very little without it.
PAYMENT_VERIFICATION=off

# The ERC-20 payments settle in. Required when verification is on.
#
# Arc USDC: 0x3600000000000000000000000000000000000000, verified on testnet
# 21 Sep 2026 — name and symbol "USDC", 6 decimals, ~3.6KB of code, and it emits
# ordinary Transfer events (19,327 in a 5,000-block sample). Arc's native gas
# balance and this ERC-20 view are the same balance, not two, so a payment here
# is a payment in the chain's own money.
#
# Nothing in payments.js is asset-specific: it matches log.address against this
# value and reads the standard Transfer topics, so switching settlement to USDC
# is configuration, not a code change. Verified end to end against a real
# 2,548.689213 USDC transfer on Arc.
PAYMENT_ASSET=

# Smallest payment that counts, per sender, in the asset's own base units (USDC has 6
# decimals, so 10000 is one cent). Stops dust from minting attestations. Must be above 0
# when PAYMENT_VERIFICATION=required: the process refuses to start otherwise. A zero-value
# transfer never counts, whatever this is.
PAYMENT_MIN_AMOUNT=10000

# Confirmations before a settlement is accepted. Raise it on a chain where
# reorgs are a real risk.
PAYMENT_MIN_CONFIRMATIONS=1

# Base units of volume per point of feeScore, capped at 20 points (MAX_FEE_SCORE;
# it was 30 before the 20 Sep reweight).
#
# 100000000 with 6-decimal USDC is one point per $100, so the cap lands at $2,000
# of settled volume. Calibrate this before settling in USDC: a single real payment
# observed on Arc was 2,548 USDC, which earns that payer's whole cap on its own
# (PAYMENT_MAX_PER_PAYER, 4 points). A payer that fills its share with one invoice
# measures "has this counterparty ever paid" rather than how much it trades, and five
# such payers reach the ceiling. Sized for a faucet token, it was never tested against
# real money.
PAYMENT_FEE_UNIT=100000000

# Days after which a payment counts half. Without decay, volume accumulates
# forever: an agent that did $3,000 of business last year still scores full marks
# today having done nothing since, and a one-off burst of farmed volume is
# permanent. Decay makes the score track current behaviour and makes farming an
# ongoing cost. Applies to successScore too, so a stale run of successes stops
# masking recent failures. Set 0 to disable.
PAYMENT_HALF_LIFE_DAYS=90

# Half-life for community flags, in days. Flags were the one signal that never
# decayed: one raised a year ago cost the same two points as one raised this
# morning. The argument for decaying evidence runs in reverse for a penalty — an
# agent that has behaved for months should stop paying for a single old flag, and a
# watchdog feed that misfires should not mark someone permanently. Shorter than the
# payment half-life by default because a flag is an accusation nobody has had to
# substantiate. Set 0 to disable decay and restore the old permanent behaviour.
FLAG_HALF_LIFE_DAYS=30

# How much evidence one counterparty can contribute: at most this many points of
# feeScore, and this many attestations of weight. Without a cap, one wallet paying
# ten times counts exactly as much as ten wallets paying once, so a small ring of
# addresses is worth as much as a real customer base. At the default of 4,
# reaching the 20-point cap needs at least five distinct payers. Set 0 to disable.
# A blank value means unset (the default), not 0.
#
# Payments from the agent's own operator or agent address are always refused,
# whatever this is set to.
PAYMENT_MAX_PER_PAYER=4

# Web of trust. A counterparty with outside standing is better evidence than an
# anonymous wallet, so its cap above is raised in proportion to that standing: at
# 1.0 a counterparty with full ERC-8004 reputation counts double. It also feeds
# propagationScore, one point per fully trusted counterparty up to five.
#
# "Standing" here is the HARD part only: ERC-8004 reputation, capped by the
# matured total. Not the counterparty's Sigvara score. Weighting by the total let
# a farmed number launder into someone else's: a sybil that wash-traded itself to
# 100 doubled its target's cap, so four scored payers maxed the 30-point fee
# factor where six are meant to be needed. Measured in oracle/adversarial.test.js.
#
# Counterparty scores are the matured ones, which lag what has just been earned,
# so a reciprocal pair cannot lift each other in a single epoch. A ring of fresh
# agents all score 0 and grant each other nothing, which is the property that
# makes this worth having: a web of trust that bootstrapped from nothing would be
# worse than none. Set 0 to weight every payer alike.
PAYMENT_TRUST_WEIGHT=1

# Optional: SigvaraEpochFees registry address. When set AND its on-chain
# epochFee > 0, the oracle only scores agents with fee coverage and charges one
# epoch fee per agent it scores (tokenomics §4). Leave unset to score every agent
# (testnet default). The oracle wallet must hold ORACLE_ROLE on the registry.
FEE_REGISTRY_ADDRESS=

# Optional: ERC-8004 registries for the externalScore factor (max 25). When all
# three are set, an agent LINKED to an ERC-8004 identity it owns (POST /link)
# gets an external-trust score computed from its 8004 feedback (see external.js).
# Unset leaves externalScore at 0.
#
# Point this at Arc, not Base Sepolia. ERC-8004 deploys its canonical singletons
# deterministically, so the two addresses below are the same on both chains and only the
# RPC decides which one is read. Both were verified live on Arc testnet, and the
# reputation registry answers the exact ABI this oracle calls.
#
# Arc is the better side of that choice for one reason: an agent that already works on
# Arc can register an 8004 identity on the chain it is already on, paying gas in the
# same USDC it settles in. Pointing at Base Sepolia asks it to go and be somebody on a
# chain it has no other reason to touch, which is why externalScore has sat at 0.
#
# A LINK IS CHAIN-IMPLICIT. /link stores a bare agentId, so it means "agent #N on
# whatever chain this RPC points at". Changing this line re-points every existing link
# at a different chain's agent #N. That is contained rather than dangerous, because
# externalScoreFor verifies ownerOf(agentId) == operator before reading any feedback, so
# a link that does not survive the move scores 0 instead of reading a stranger's
# standing. Re-link affected agents after changing it.
#
# Arc mainnet (5042) carries the same pair at different addresses:
#   identity   0x8004a169fb4a3325136eb29fa0ceb6d2e539a432
#   reputation 0x8004BAa17C55a88189AE136b182e5fdA19dE9b63
EXTERNAL_RPC=https://rpc.testnet.arc.io
EXTERNAL_IDENTITY_ADDRESS=0x8004A818BFB912233c491871b3d84c89A494BD9e
EXTERNAL_REPUTATION_ADDRESS=0x8004B663056A597Dffe9eCcC1965A193B7388713

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Payment evidence by pull (ADR 0003) — OFF by default
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Each operator finds payments by scanning ERC-20 Transfer logs to registered agent
# addresses, instead of waiting for somebody to POST the settlement hash to it.
#
# Verification was always chain-based; only the notification was not. With two operators
# that difference stopped being invisible: six payers paid the demo agent, every
# settlement was attested to the primary, and the checker scored 23 SVR against the
# primary's 3,023. Nobody misbehaved. The evidence never arrived, because arrival was
# per-operator and by HTTP.
#
# A pulled payment is credited with NO outcome. Money moving is on chain; whether the
# work was good is not, and never will be. It counts toward fee volume and tenure and is
# excluded from both sides of the success ratio. Outcomes stay push, via /attest.
#
# Turning this on changes scores, because an operator that has been missing payments
# starts counting them. That is the intent, and it is why it is off by default rather
# than something that happens by surprise. Enable it on BOTH operators or they will
# diverge for the opposite reason to the one this fixes.
PAYMENT_SCAN_ENABLED=0
# Where to start when there is no checkpoint. Defaults to FROM_BLOCK. Scanning from
# genesis on a busy chain is the fastest way to be rate-limited into never finishing.
PAYMENT_SCAN_FROM_BLOCK=
# Defaults to LOG_CHUNK_SIZE.
PAYMENT_SCAN_CHUNK=
# Blocks to stay behind the head. Floor of 6, and an explicit value may raise it but not
# go below it. Deeper than the attested path on purpose: an attestation is a deliberate
# act by someone who watched the transaction settle, whereas a scan credits unattended,
# every epoch, with nobody looking. A credit records the tx hash as used, so a reorg
# would leave a credit for a transaction that no longer exists.
PAYMENT_SCAN_CONFIRMATIONS=
# Milliseconds between getLogs calls. Both Arc endpoints limit with a bucket of about 60
# requests, so back-to-back calls are refused most of the time and a long catch-up becomes a
# coin flip. The default of 500 sustained 72 consecutive calls when measured. Costs nothing in
# steady running, where an epoch is one call. 0 opts out.
PAYMENT_SCAN_PACE_MS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ERC-8183 job registry (Agentic Commerce) — READ ONLY, off by default
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# An ERC-8183 job is escrow with a verdict: a client funds it, a provider submits work,
# and a named evaluator calls complete() or reject(). That verdict is the one input this
# oracle has never been able to check for itself. Today "it went well" arrives inside an
# attestation and is taken on trust; under ERC-8183 it is a log entry written by an
# address that is neither the agent nor, usually, the party paying for the work.
#
# Nothing here feeds a score. Setting these enables GET /jobs/<address>, which reports
# what the registry says and nothing else. Two reasons, both of which resolve with time:
#
#   - ERC-8183 is a Draft ERC (2026-02-25). A draft can change an event signature, and an
#     indexer pinned to one that moved does not fail loudly: it matches nothing and
#     reports every agent as having done no work.
#   - There is no canonical deployment on Arc. The contracts found there are individual
#     demos, quiet for thousands of blocks.
#
# Unset leaves the reader disabled, and /jobs answers 501 rather than an empty list. An
# empty list would read as "this agent has done no jobs", which is the opposite of
# "nobody has told this oracle where to look".
JOBS_REGISTRY_ADDRESS=
# Defaults to RPC_URL. Set only if the registry lives on a different chain.
JOBS_RPC=
JOBS_FROM_BLOCK=0
# Widest block range one request may scan, so an unbounded scan from genesis is not
# something a stranger can ask this process to do.
JOBS_MAX_SPAN=50000
# Blocks per getLogs call when reading the registry. Defaults to LOG_CHUNK_SIZE.
JOBS_LOG_CHUNK=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Second operator: checker mode
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set ORACLE_MODE=checker to run this process as a SECOND bonded operator that
# audits the primary rather than racing it. Leave unset (or 'primary') for the
# operator that actually proposes scores. Any other value, including 'Checker' or
# 'CHECKER', stops the process: it used to start a primary.
#
# Why the distinction exists. SigvaraReputation stores ONE pending proposal per
# agent: a second proposeReputation overwrites the first and restarts its
# challenge window, and finalizeReputation only asks that the window elapsed
# unchallenged. There is no quorum, no median, no vote. So two operators both
# proposing would not produce agreement, they would produce a race, and the
# loser's opinion would be silently discarded.
#
# A checker therefore never competes for the slot:
#   nothing pending              -> proposes (covers for a silent primary)
#   pending, agrees, window open -> does nothing
#   pending, agrees, window past -> finalizes
#   pending, disagrees           -> records a divergence and touches NOTHING
#
# That last prohibition is the point. Overwriting a disputed proposal restarts
# the challenge window and buys it another six hours beyond the reach of
# SLASHING_COMMITTEE_ROLE, which holds the only power to reject it. The
# committee has always had that power and has never had anything telling it
# when to use it; divergences are that signal, readable at GET /divergence.
#
# Run it on different hardware and a different RPC endpoint from the primary.
# A checker sharing a host and a chain view with what it checks mostly proves
# that the code is deterministic.
#
# It needs its own wallet, its own 25,000 SVR bond, its own admission by
# DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE on SigvaraOracleBond, and its own state file.
#
# A checker must run with FEE_REGISTRY_ADDRESS UNSET: the primary bills, and a second
# operator running the fee path would charge the agent twice on a registry from before the
# once-per-epoch charge, or race the primary for the one charge a current one allows.
# Startup refuses the combination. And EPOCH_HOURS must be below half the challenge
# window (under 3 on Arc testnet), or proposals expire before the checker ever sees them.
ORACLE_MODE=
# Total score points of disagreement tolerated before it is called a divergence.
# Two honest operators scanning at different moments differ by a point or two: a
# payment lands between their scans, or an age bucket ticks over. Alerting on
# that would train the committee to ignore alerts. Applied to the total AND to
# any single factor, so offsetting errors cannot cancel into apparent agreement.
#
# Validated at startup and capped at 10. Number('3 points') is NaN, every comparison
# against NaN is false, and the comparisons here are all '>' — so an unvalidated
# tolerance would not make the checker noisy, it would make it agree with every proposal
# on chain. A large finite value does the same and looks more plausible in a config file.
DIVERGENCE_TOLERANCE=3

# --- which code runs (docker-compose.*.vps.yml only) ----------------------------------
# The full 40-character hash of a commit on main that someone has read, never a branch name.
# The VPS compose files fetch exactly this commit when the container starts and refuse to
# start on anything else. Use the same hash on the primary, the checker and the watcher.
# A deploy is: change it, then `docker compose up -d` (a plain restart does not re-read .env).
# Take the hash from `git rev-parse origin/main` in a checkout you have read.
#
# SIGVARA_COMMIT=

# --- payer-signed outcomes (docs/adr/0006-payer-signed-outcomes.md) ------------------------
# The payer of a payment signs whether the work was delivered (EIP-712), so its outcome needs no
# token. The README's configuration table has each setting in full. The testnet operators run:
#   PAYER_OUTCOMES=positive       off (default), positive (only "delivered") or both
#   PAYER_OUTCOMES_PUBLISH=on     serve the signed payloads in /evidence and at GET /outcomes
#   PAYER_OUTCOMES_PEERS=<the other operator's base URL>, comma-separated for several
# Defaults that apply when unset:
#   PAYER_OUTCOMES_IMPORT_GRACE_HOURS=6   how late after its deadline a peer's outcome may arrive
#   PAYER_OUTCOME_WINDOW_DAYS=14          how long a payer's outcome may replace a token holder's
#   PAYER_NEGATIVE_MIN_AMOUNT=<PAYMENT_MIN_AMOUNT>   least leg for a "not delivered" to count
# PAYER_OUTCOMES=positive
# PAYER_OUTCOMES_PUBLISH=on
# PAYER_OUTCOMES_PEERS=
